From 79fcdbb8ea38a771afa4a064660e2e3dd92a3e09 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Kuasar Sandbox <noreply@kuasar.dev>
Date: Wed, 18 Nov 2026 11:05:00 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 15/15] virtio-devices: publish vsock transport reset before freeze

Restoring a vsock device waits for the guest to acknowledge the
TRANSPORT_RESET event staged while the VM was paused for the snapshot, so the
restore dial pays the guest's whole reset processing time (60-70 ms) before
the first connection can be established.

Publish the reset while the VM is still running instead, after the host has
quiesced the guest and before /vm.pause. The vsock worker owns the live event
queue, memory and queue eventfds, so the VMM thread only writes a command
eventfd and polls shared atomics. The worker drains stale kicks, pops an event
descriptor, writes VIRTIO_VSOCK_EVENT_TRANSPORT_RESET, adds it to the used
ring, sets transport_reset_pending and signals the queue.

The guest acknowledges by advancing the event queue avail index and kicking,
so a stale kick alone cannot acknowledge the reset. snapshot() skips staging
when a reset was already published and serializes the current pending state:
a timed out reset still produces a gated snapshot that the existing restore
path understands. Nothing new is serialized: reset_published is runtime only
and is cleared by activate/resume/reset.

The worker and the snapshot path share one event queue instance so their
next_avail/next_used bookkeeping cannot diverge after a running reset followed
by a source resume and another snapshot.

Expose the capability as PUT /api/v1/vm.vsock-reset with an optional
{"timeout_ms": N} body, returning {"published": bool, "acked": bool}. The
request is idempotent and never publishes twice, and a missing or inactive
device reports published=false.

Add KUASAR_TEST_HOOKS=1 failure injection (delay-ack, drop-ack, no-desc,
fail-after-publish, stale-kick) for the negative tests. Release binaries
ignore the hooks unless built with the `kuasar-test-hooks` feature.
